// U1 L3 Circular and Projectile Motion — 25-question bank
// Moodle quiz: draw 5 random questions; shuffle choices; one attempt; pass 80%.

::PHY-U1-L3-Q01::A projectile is an object that, after launch, moves primarily under the influence of {=gravity#Correct. Gravity produces vertical acceleration.~air resistance alone#Air resistance is not the defining interaction in the ideal model.~its horizontal velocity#Velocity describes motion but does not cause acceleration.~centripetal force#Centripetal force applies to circular motion.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q02::Ignoring air resistance, which quantity remains constant for a projectile? {~vertical velocity#Gravity continually changes vertical velocity.=horizontal velocity#Correct. With no horizontal net force, horizontal velocity is constant.~total velocity#Its direction and magnitude generally change.~vertical displacement#Vertical position changes during flight.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q03::At the highest point of an ideal projectile's path, its vertical velocity is {~equal to g#g is acceleration, not velocity.~at its maximum#The vertical component has decreased to zero.=zero#Correct. The projectile changes from rising to falling.~equal to its horizontal velocity#The two components are independent.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q04::Throughout ideal projectile motion near Earth's surface, vertical acceleration is {~zero#Gravity still acts at the highest point.~upward at 9.8 m/s²#Gravity acts downward.~different for rising and falling motion#The acceleration remains constant.=downward at approximately 9.8 m/s²#Correct.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q05::Why is a projectile's trajectory curved? {=Horizontal and vertical motions occur simultaneously.#Correct. Constant horizontal motion combines with accelerated vertical motion.~Its horizontal acceleration constantly increases.#Horizontal acceleration is zero in the ideal model.~Gravity acts horizontally and vertically.#Gravity acts vertically downward.~Its mass changes while it moves.#Mass does not change.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q06::A ball rolls horizontally off a table. Compared with a ball dropped from rest at the same instant and height, it lands {~later because it travels farther#Horizontal distance does not change fall time.~earlier because it is already moving#Initial horizontal velocity does not affect vertical fall time.~only if both balls have the same mass#Ideal gravitational acceleration is independent of mass.=at the same time#Correct. Their vertical motions are identical.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q07::Which pair correctly describes the velocity of an object in uniform circular motion? {~Constant magnitude and constant direction#Direction changes continuously.=Constant magnitude and changing direction#Correct.~Changing magnitude and constant direction#Uniform circular motion has constant speed.~Zero magnitude and changing direction#The object is moving.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q08::The instantaneous velocity of an object in circular motion points {~toward the center#That is the centripetal-acceleration direction.~away from the center#Velocity is not radial.=tangent to the circle#Correct.~opposite the centripetal acceleration only at the top#Velocity is perpendicular to centripetal acceleration everywhere.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q09::Centripetal acceleration points {~tangent to the path#That is the velocity direction.~opposite the velocity#It is perpendicular, not opposite.~away from the center#That would be outward.=toward the center#Correct.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q10::An object moving at constant speed in a circle is accelerating because {=its velocity direction changes#Correct. Acceleration is a change in velocity.~its mass changes#Mass remains constant.~its radius must increase#The radius may remain constant.~its speed continually increases#Uniform circular motion has constant speed.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q11::For fixed speed, decreasing the radius of a circular path makes centripetal acceleration {~decrease#The inverse relationship gives the opposite result.=increase#Correct: a_c = v²/r.~remain zero#Circular motion requires inward acceleration.~remain unchanged#Radius appears in the denominator.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q12::For fixed radius, doubling circular speed changes centripetal acceleration by a factor of {~2#Acceleration depends on speed squared.~1/2#The acceleration increases, not decreases.~8#The exponent is two.=4#Correct.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q13::A vector is resolved into components to {~change its physical meaning#Components describe the same vector.~eliminate its direction#Direction information is preserved.=analyze perpendicular contributions independently#Correct.~make every component equal#Component magnitudes depend on direction.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q14::A projectile launched at speed v and angle θ has horizontal component {~v sin θ#This is the vertical component for the usual angle definition.~v tan θ#Tangent gives a component ratio.~v²/r#That is associated with centripetal acceleration.=v cos θ#Correct.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q15::The vertical component of a launch velocity v at angle θ above horizontal is {=v sin θ#Correct.~v cos θ#That is the horizontal component.~g sin θ#Gravity is acceleration, not launch speed.~v/θ#Angles are not used this way.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q16::Two ideal projectiles are launched from level ground at equal speed using complementary angles. They have the same {~maximum height#Higher launch angle has greater vertical component.~flight time#Higher vertical component produces longer time aloft.=range#Correct in the level-ground ideal model.~initial vertical velocity#Complementary angles generally have different sine values.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q17::Which change increases the time of flight for an ideal projectile launched and landing at the same height? {~Increase only its horizontal component#Fall time depends on vertical motion.=Increase its initial vertical component#Correct.~Decrease gravitational acceleration while holding all else fixed only if it is rising#A smaller g increases the entire flight time; the qualifier is wrong.~Increase its mass#Mass does not affect ideal flight time.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q18::Relative velocity depends on {~only the object's mass#Mass is irrelevant to the reference description.~only Earth's gravitational field#Reference frame is the key.=the observer's reference frame#Correct.~whether motion is circular#All motion can be described relative to a frame.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q19::A person walks 2 m/s east on a sidewalk moving 3 m/s east relative to the ground. The person's ground velocity is {~1 m/s west#Directions are the same, so magnitudes add.~1 m/s east#That would result from subtraction.~6 m/s east#The speeds add, not multiply.=5 m/s east#Correct.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q20::A boat moves 4 m/s north relative to water while current moves 3 m/s east. Its ground-speed magnitude is {=5 m/s#Correct by the Pythagorean theorem.~7 m/s#That would add perpendicular magnitudes directly.~1 m/s#That would subtract them.~12 m/s#That multiplies them.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q21::Which graph feature best supports constant horizontal velocity for a projectile? {~Horizontal position is constant#That would mean zero horizontal velocity.~Horizontal acceleration increases#The ideal model has zero horizontal acceleration.=Horizontal position changes by equal amounts in equal times#Correct.~Vertical position changes equally in equal times#Vertical motion is accelerated.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q22::At the top of an ideal projectile's path, which statement is correct? {~Both velocity components are zero#Horizontal velocity remains nonzero.~Acceleration is zero#Gravity still acts.~Horizontal acceleration equals g#Gravity is vertical.=Horizontal velocity is nonzero and vertical velocity is zero#Correct.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q23::Which model limitation is most important when applying ideal projectile equations to a lightweight ball outdoors? {~The equations include too much horizontal acceleration#They assume zero horizontal acceleration.=Air resistance and wind may be significant#Correct.~Gravity has no vertical effect#Gravity is central to the model.~Mass must continually increase#Mass is essentially constant.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q24::A satellite in a circular orbit has an instantaneous velocity tangent to the orbit and acceleration {~tangent in the same direction#That would change speed rather than direction.~tangent in the opposite direction#That would slow it down.=toward the center of the orbit#Correct.~zero because speed is constant#Changing direction requires acceleration.}

::PHY-U1-L3-Q25::Which comparison is accurate? {~Both projectile and circular motion have constant velocity#Both have changing velocity.=Both can have changing velocity even when one component or the speed is constant#Correct.~Only projectile motion involves acceleration#Circular motion has centripetal acceleration.~Only circular motion can be represented with vectors#Both require vector representations.}
